```cpp
struct Version {
    uint64_t commit_timestamp;
    std::string value;
    std::atomic<Version*> next;
};

struct Node {
    KeyType key; // Order-preserving
    std::atomic<Version*> version_chain;
    // Other B+-Tree metadata
};

void insert_version(Node* node, uint64_t commit_ts, std::string value) {
    Version* new_version = new Version{commit_ts, value, nullptr};
    Version* head;
    do {
        head = node->version_chain.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        new_version->next = head;
    } while (!node->version_chain.compare_exchange_weak(
        head, new_version, std::memory_order_release, std::memory_order_relaxed));
}
```

**Challenges**:
- Avoid contention on the version chain during high write loads by minimizing hot-path atomic operations.
- Garbage collection (see below for EBR) ensures retired versions are safely reclaimed.

---

#### 2. Garbage Collection with Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR)

**Core Idea**:
- All deleted objects are added to a thread-private **retirement list** with its retirement epoch tagged.
- Physical memory reclaiming occurs only after no thread is accessing any epoch earlier than the retirement epoch of the object.

**Implementation Steps**:
1. Use atomic counters to track a **global epoch** and **per-thread epochs**.
2. When a thread completes a quiescent period (e.g., transaction commit), it announces its epoch.
3. Periodically, the oldest epoch in use across all threads is determined. Safely retire objects whose retirement epoch is older.

```cpp
class EpochReclamation {
public:
    void enter_critical() noexcept {
        local_epoch = global_epoch.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
    }

    void exit_critical() noexcept {
        local_epoch = std::nullopt;
    }

    void retire(void* object) {
        retired_objects.emplace_back(object, global_epoch);
        if (retired_objects.size() > threshold) {
            collect_garbage();
        }
    }

    void collect_garbage() {
        uint64_t min_epoch = compute_min_epoch();
        auto it = retired_objects.begin();
        while (it != retired_objects.end() && it->second < min_epoch) {
            delete it->first;
            it = retired_objects.erase(it);
        }
    }

private:
    static thread_local std::optional<uint64_t> local_epoch;
    static std::atomic<uint64_t> global_epoch;
    std::vector<std::pair<void*, uint64_t>> retired_objects;
};
```

**Trade-offs**:
- Epoch-based reclamation has faster amortized performance than hazard pointers but can lead to longer retention of garbage under bursty workloads.

