Your problem involves a critical data race issue in a low-latency transactional storage engine under extreme throughput. This is further complicated by the combination of concurrent writers, readers, and rollback threads operating under Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR). The solution requires a deep integration of modern C++ memory management constructs, efficient synchronization primitives, and NUMA-aware infrastructure to guarantee correctness.

Below is the design and a simplified implementation of the requested core subsystem in C++20. We will carefully address each requirement you listed.

---

### **Memory Model & Epoch Reclamation Architecture**

#### Design:
1. **Thread Registration and Epoch Management:**
   Each thread must register and deregister with the Epoch Manager. The Epoch Manager will hold a per-thread metadata structure for tracking quiescent states and associated epochs.

2. **Node Retirement:**
   Retired nodes will be placed into per-thread retired lists. Epoch advancement will batch reclaim these nodes only when no active thread is referencing older epochs.

3. **Precise Synchronization:**
   Acquire-Release semantics (`std::memory_order_acquire`, `std::memory_order_release`) will be used for all epoch visibility and node pointer updates. CAS operations for structural changes like `install_consolidated_node` will use `std::memory_order_acq_rel`.

#### Code:

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <vector>
#include <thread>
#include <cassert>
#include <iostream>
#include <array>
#include <memory_resource>

constexpr size_t MAX_THREADS = 128;
constexpr size_t CACHE_LINE_SIZE = 64;

struct EpochEntry {
    std::atomic<size_t> current_epoch;
    std::atomic<bool> active; // Indicates if thread is registered and active.
    alignas(CACHE_LINE_SIZE) char padding[CACHE_LINE_SIZE];
};

class EpochManager {
private:
    std::atomic<size_t> global_epoch;
    std::array<EpochEntry, MAX_THREADS> thread_epochs;
    std::vector<void*> retired_nodes[MAX_THREADS];

public:
    EpochManager() : global_epoch(0) {
        for (auto& entry : thread_epochs) {
            entry.current_epoch.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
            entry.active.store(false, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        }
    }

    size_t register_thread() {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; ++i) {
            if (!thread_epochs[i].active.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) {
                thread_epochs[i].active.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
                return i;
            }
        }
        throw std::runtime_error("Max threads exceeded");
    }

    void deregister_thread(size_t thread_id) {
        thread_epochs[thread_id].active.store(false, std::memory_order_release);
    }

    void enter_epoch(size_t thread_id) {
        thread_epochs[thread_id].current_epoch.store(global_epoch.load(std::memory_order_acquire), std::memory_order_release);
    }

    void leave_epoch(size_t thread_id) {
        thread_epochs[thread_id].current_epoch.store(0, std::memory_order_release);
    }

    void retire_node(size_t thread_id, void* node) {
        retired_nodes[thread_id].push_back(node);
    }

    void garbage_collect() {
        size_t min_epoch = global_epoch.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        for (const auto& entry : thread_epochs) {
            if (entry.active.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
                size_t epoch = entry.current_epoch.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
                if (epoch < min_epoch && epoch > 0) {
                    min_epoch = epoch;
                }
            }
        }

        // Increment global epoch for the next round.
        global_epoch.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_acq_rel);

        // Reclaim memory from previous epochs
        for (size_t i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; ++i) {
            for (void* node : retired_nodes[i]) {
                // Node can be safely deleted if it predates the min_epoch.
                // (An actual production implementation needs better epoch tagging for safe deletion.)
                // This is simplified.
                delete static_cast<char*>(node); 
            }
            retired_nodes[i].clear();
        }
    }
};
