### Architecture & Memory Model Rationale

#### 1. Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR) Synchronization Protocol

On weakly ordered architectures (ARM64 / aarch64), memory operations can be reordered aggressively:
- Stores can be reordered after loads ($S \rightarrow L$).
- Stores can be reordered after stores ($S \rightarrow S$).
- Loads can be reordered after loads ($L \rightarrow L$) and loads after stores ($L \rightarrow S$).

The classic hazard in EBR occurs when a thread announces its entry into an epoch (writing to its thread-local active state) and then dereferences a pointer to a node (reading from shared memory). On ARM64, without proper synchronization, the read of the shared pointer could be reordered **before** the write to the thread-local epoch flag becomes globally visible. As a result, a concurrent thread advancing the epoch and checking active thread states could observe the reader as *inactive*, advance the global epoch through all 3 cycles ($e \rightarrow e+1 \rightarrow e+2 \pmod 3$), and physically reclaim (`free`) the node while the reader is actively accessing it.

```
       Thread A (Consumer / Accessor)             Thread B (Reclaimer / Advancer)
       ------------------------------             -------------------------------
(1) LocalEpoch.store(Active | GlobalEpoch)
    [ Full Fence / seq_cst / membarrier ]
(2) Node* p = QueueHead.load(acquire)
(3) Access p->data ...                     (4) CanAdvanceEpoch? -> Reads all LocalEpochs
                                           (5) GlobalEpoch.store(next, seq_cst)
                                           (6) Reclaim nodes from 2 epochs ago
```

##### Happens-Before Graph for EBR Lifecycle
1. **Critical Section Entry**:
   $$\text{LocalEpoch.store}(\text{Active} \mid E_{\text{global}}, \text{memory\_order\_seq\_cst})$$
   A sequentially consistent store (or `std::atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_seq_cst)`) creates a full hardware barrier (`dmb ish` on ARM64). This ensures:
   $$\text{LocalEpoch Store} \prec_{\text{program order}} \text{Queue Read} \implies \text{LocalEpoch Store} \prec_{\text{observed}} \text{Advancement Scan}$$
2. **Node Retirement**:
   A node is retired into a per-thread retirement bag tagged with the current global epoch $E_{\text{retire}}$.
3. **Epoch Advancement & Reclamation**:
   To advance global epoch $E \to (E + 1) \pmod 3$, the advancer must read the local epoch of all active registered threads with `memory_order_seq_cst`.
   If for all threads $T_i$, $\text{epoch}(T_i) = \text{Inactive} \lor \text{epoch}(T_i) = E$, the global epoch is advanced to $E' = (E + 1) \pmod 3$.
   Nodes retired at epoch $E_{\text{retire}}$ are safely freed when the global epoch reaches $E_{\text{free}} = (E_{\text{retire}} + 2) \pmod 3$, because all threads that could have observed the node in $E_{\text{retire}}$ must have either exited their critical sections or advanced to at least $E_{\text{retire}} + 1$.

---

#### 2. Segmented Lock-Free MPMC Queue Slot State Machine

Each `Segment` is an unrolled ring buffer of fixed capacity $N$ (e.g., $N = 1024$). Every slot $i \in [0, N-1]$ contains:
- `std::atomic<size_t> turn`: The slot sequence counter initialized to $i$.
- `alignas(alignof(T)) std::byte storage[sizeof(T)]`: Uninitialized storage for $T$.

```
 Slot Sequence State Transitions for Slot i (Capacity N, Cycle Count k):

   [ Initial State: turn = 0*N + i ]
                 │
                 │  Producer CAS/fetch_add ticket = k*N + i
                 │  Awaits: turn == 2*k*N + i
                 ▼
         [ Write Value T ]  (Construct in-place)
                 │
                 │  turn.store(2*k*N + i + 1, release)
                 ▼
   [ Readable State: turn = 2*k*N + i + 1 ]
                 │
                 │  Consumer CAS/fetch_add ticket = k*N + i
                 │  Awaits: turn == 2*k*N + i + 1
                 ▼
         [ Read & Move Value T ] (Destruct in-place)
                 │
                 │  turn.store(2*(k+1)*N + i, release)
                 ▼
   [ Recycled State: turn = 2*(k+1)*N + i ]
```

##### Handling Producer/Consumer Lag and Dynamic Segment Transitions
- When `tail_idx` reaches segment capacity $N$, producers atomically allocate and link a new segment:
  $$\text{tail\_segment}\to\text{next.compare\_exchange\_strong}(\text{nullptr}, \text{new\_segment}, \text{release}, \text{acquire})$$
- Even if a slow producer holds a slot index in an older segment and stalls, EBR prevents the older segment from being freed until the slow producer finishes and drops its `EpochGuard`.
- When all $N$ slots of a segment have been consumed (i.e., consumer ticket reaches $N$), the consumer atomically advances `head_segment` to `head_segment->next.load(acquire)` and passes the depleted segment to `EpochManager::retire()`.

---

### Complete C++20 Header-Only Implementation

```cpp
#pragma once

#include <atomic>
#include <cassert>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <memory>
#include <new>
#include <optional>
#include <thread>
#include <type_traits>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

#ifndef __cpp_lib_hardware_interference_size
namespace std {
    constexpr std::size_t hardware_destructive_interference_size = 64;
    constexpr std::size_t hardware_constructive_interference_size = 64;
}
#endif

// ============================================================================
// 1. Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR) Subsystem
// ============================================================================

namespace ebr {

constexpr uint64_t INACTIVE_EPOCH = uint64_t(1) << 62;
constexpr uint64_t EPOCH_MASK     = 0x3; // 3-epoch scheme (0, 1, 2)
constexpr size_t   NUM_EPOCHS     = 3;
constexpr size_t   RETIRE_BATCH_THRESHOLD = 64;

class EpochManager;

// Thread-local EBR control block
struct alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) ThreadControlBlock {
    // Bits 0-1: Observed global epoch. Bit 62: Inactive flag.
    std::atomic<uint64_t> local_epoch{INACTIVE_EPOCH};

    // Per-thread limbo bags for each epoch (0, 1, 2)
    struct RetiredNode {
        void* data{nullptr};
        void (*deleter)(void*){nullptr};
        RetiredNode* next{nullptr};
    };

    struct LimboBag {
        RetiredNode* head{nullptr};
        size_t count{0};
    };

    LimboBag bags[NUM_EPOCHS];
    size_t total_retired_count{0};

    std::atomic<ThreadControlBlock*> next{nullptr};
    std::atomic<bool> is_registered{true};
    EpochManager* manager{nullptr};

    ThreadControlBlock() = default;
    ~ThreadControlBlock();

    void retire(void* ptr, void (*deleter)(void*), uint64_t current_global_epoch);
    void reclaim_epoch_bag(size_t epoch_idx);
    void drain_all_bags();
};

class EpochManager {
public:
    static EpochManager& instance() {
        static EpochManager mgr;
        return mgr;
    }

    EpochManager() : global_epoch_(0), thread_list_head_(nullptr) {}

    ~EpochManager() {
        // Collect and drain remaining retired objects across all threads
        ThreadControlBlock* curr = thread_list_head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        while (curr != nullptr) {
            curr->drain_all_bags();
            curr = curr->next.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        }
    }

    EpochManager(const EpochManager&) = delete;
    EpochManager& operator=(const EpochManager&) = delete;

    uint64_t get_global_epoch() const noexcept {
        return global_epoch_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    }

    void register_thread(ThreadControlBlock* tcb) {
        tcb->manager = this;
        tcb->is_registered.store(true, std::memory_order_release);

        ThreadControlBlock* old_head = thread_list_head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        do {
            tcb->next.store(old_head, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        } while (!thread_list_head_.compare_exchange_weak(
            old_head, tcb, std::memory_order_release, std::memory_order_relaxed));
    }

    void unregister_thread(ThreadControlBlock* tcb) {
        // Thread marks itself inactive and drops registered status
        tcb->local_epoch.store(INACTIVE_EPOCH, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
        tcb->is_registered.store(false, std::memory_order_release);

        // Pass any remaining deferred nodes to another active epoch or drain
        try_advance_and_reclaim(tcb);
        tcb->drain_all_bags();
    }

    bool try_advance_epoch() {
        uint64_t current = global_epoch_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);

        // Full barrier to ensure subsequent TCB reads do not pass previous operations
        std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_seq_cst);

        ThreadControlBlock* curr = thread_list_head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        while (curr != nullptr) {
            if (curr->is_registered.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
                uint64_t local = curr->local_epoch.load(std::memory_order_seq_cst);
                // If the thread is active and in an older epoch, we cannot advance
                if ((local & INACTIVE_EPOCH) == 0) {
                    uint64_t thread_epoch = local & EPOCH_MASK;
                    if (thread_epoch != current) {
                        return false;
                    }
                }
            }
            curr = curr->next.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        }

        uint64_t next_epoch = (current + 1) % NUM_EPOCHS;
        return global_epoch_.compare_exchange_strong(
            current, next_epoch, std::memory_order_seq_cst, std::memory_order_relaxed);
    }

    void try_advance_and_reclaim(ThreadControlBlock* tcb) {
        if (try_advance_epoch()) {
            uint64_t new_global = global_epoch_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
            // In a 3-epoch scheme (0, 1, 2), nodes retired at (new_global + 1) % 3
            // are guaranteed to be unreferenced now.
            size_t reclaim_epoch = (new_global + 1) % NUM_EPOCHS;
            tcb->reclaim_epoch_bag(reclaim_epoch);
        }
    }

private:
    alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<uint64_t> global_epoch_;
    alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<ThreadControlBlock*> thread_list_head_;
};

inline thread_local ThreadControlBlock tls_tcb;

inline ThreadControlBlock::~ThreadControlBlock() {
    if (manager) {
        manager->unregister_thread(this);
    }
}

inline void ThreadControlBlock::retire(void* ptr, void (*deleter)(void*), uint64_t current_global_epoch) {
    size_t epoch_idx = current_global_epoch % NUM_EPOCHS;
    auto* node = new RetiredNode{ptr, deleter, bags[epoch_idx].head};
    bags[epoch_idx].head = node;
    bags[epoch_idx].count++;
    total_retired_count++;

    if (total_retired_count >= RETIRE_BATCH_THRESHOLD) {
        if (manager) {
            manager->try_advance_and_reclaim(this);
        }
    }
}

inline void ThreadControlBlock::reclaim_epoch_bag(size_t epoch_idx) {
    RetiredNode* curr = bags[epoch_idx].head;
    bags[epoch_idx].head = nullptr;
    total_retired_count -= bags[epoch_idx].count;
    bags[epoch_idx].count = 0;

    while (curr != nullptr) {
        RetiredNode* next = curr->next;
        if (curr->deleter && curr->data) {
            curr->deleter(curr->data);
        }
        delete curr;
        curr = next;
    }
}

inline void ThreadControlBlock::drain_all_bags() {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < NUM_EPOCHS; ++i) {
        reclaim_epoch_bag(i);
    }
}

// RAII EBR Guard for critical sections
class Guard {
public:
    Guard() noexcept {
        tcb_ = &tls_tcb;
        if (__builtin_expect(!initialized_, false)) {
            EpochManager::instance().register_thread(tcb_);
            initialized_ = true;
        }

        uint64_t global_epoch = EpochManager::instance().get_global_epoch();
        // ARM64 Critical: seq_cst store ensures epoch publication is visible
        // BEFORE any speculative pointer dereferencing occurs in the critical section.
        tcb_->local_epoch.store(global_epoch, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
    }

    ~Guard() noexcept {
        tcb_->local_epoch.store(INACTIVE_EPOCH, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
    }

    Guard(const Guard&) = delete;
    Guard& operator=(const Guard&) = delete;
    Guard(Guard&&) = delete;
    Guard& operator=(Guard&&) = delete;

    template <typename T>
    void retire(T* ptr) noexcept {
        if (!ptr) return;
        auto deleter = [](void* p) {
            delete static_cast<T*>(p);
        };
        uint64_t cur_epoch = tcb_->local_epoch.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) & EPOCH_MASK;
        tcb_->retire(ptr, deleter, cur_epoch);
    }

private:
    ThreadControlBlock* tcb_;
    inline static thread_local bool initialized_{false};
};

} // namespace ebr

// ============================================================================
// 2. Segmented Lock-Free Multi-Producer Multi-Consumer (MPMC) Queue
// ============================================================================

template <typename T, size_t SegmentSize = 1024>
class LockFreeMPMCQueue {
    static_assert(SegmentSize > 0 && ((SegmentSize & (SegmentSize - 1)) == 0),
                  "SegmentSize must be a power of two.");
    static_assert(std::is_nothrow_destructible_v<T>,
                  "T must be nothrow destructible.");

    struct Slot {
        // Even turn: slot is ready for producer turn = 2 * (seq / SegmentSize)
        // Odd turn:  slot is ready for consumer turn = 2 * (seq / SegmentSize) + 1
        alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<size_t> turn;
        alignas(alignof(T)) std::byte storage[sizeof(T)];

        Slot() noexcept : turn(0) {}

        template <typename... Args>
        void construct(Args&&... args) {
            new (static_cast<void*>(storage)) T(std::forward<Args>(args)...);
        }

        void destroy() noexcept {
            reinterpret_cast<T*>(storage)->~T();
        }

        T* value_ptr() noexcept {
            return reinterpret_cast<T*>(storage);
        }
    };

    struct Segment {
        alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<size_t> enqueue_idx{0};
        alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<size_t> dequeue_idx{0};
        alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<Segment*> next{nullptr};

        const size_t segment_id;
        Slot slots[SegmentSize];

        explicit Segment(size_t id) noexcept : segment_id(id) {
            for (size_t i = 0; i < SegmentSize; ++i) {
                // Initial sequence turn for slot i
                slots[i].turn.store(i, std::memory_order_relaxed);
            }
        }

        ~Segment() noexcept {
            // Destruction of any remaining live elements if the queue is torn down
            size_t head = dequeue_idx.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
            size_t tail = enqueue_idx.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
            size_t end = std::min(tail, SegmentSize);

            for (size_t i = head; i < end; ++i) {
                size_t slot_turn = slots[i].turn.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
                // Odd turn indicates an unconsumed constructed item in the slot
                if (slot_turn % 2 != 0) {
                    slots[i].destroy();
                }
            }
        }
    };

public:
    LockFreeMPMCQueue() {
        Segment* initial_seg = new Segment(0);
        head_segment_.store(initial_seg, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        tail_segment_.store(initial_seg, std::memory_order_relaxed);
    }

    ~LockFreeMPMCQueue() {
        // Drain remaining items without EBR guards
        T dummy;
        while (try_dequeue(dummy)) {}

        Segment* curr = head_segment_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        while (curr != nullptr) {
            Segment* next = curr->next.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
            delete curr;
            curr = next;
        }
    }

    LockFreeMPMCQueue(const LockFreeMPMCQueue&) = delete;
    LockFreeMPMCQueue& operator=(const LockFreeMPMCQueue&) = delete;

    template <typename... Args>
    void emplace(Args&&... args) {
        ebr::Guard guard;
        while (true) {
            Segment* tail = tail_segment_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
            size_t ticket = tail->enqueue_idx.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_acq_rel);

            if (ticket < SegmentSize) {
                Slot& slot = tail->slots[ticket];
                // Expected turn for slot ticket in this segment
                const size_t expected_turn = ticket;

                // Busy-wait for consumer to recycle slot (if applicable)
                while (slot.turn.load(std::memory_order_acquire) != expected_turn) {
                    #if defined(__aarch64__)
                    asm volatile("yield" ::: "memory");
                    #elif defined(__x86_64__)
                    asm volatile("pause" ::: "memory");
                    #else
                    std::this_thread::yield();
                    #endif
                }

                // Construct value in place with exception safety
                try {
                    slot.construct(std::forward<Args>(args)...);
                } catch (...) {
                    // Reset slot turn on exception to prevent producer stalls
                    slot.turn.store(expected_turn + 1, std::memory_order_release);
                    throw;
                }

                // Release: mark slot as ready for consumption
                slot.turn.store(expected_turn + 1, std::memory_order_release);
                return;
            }

            // Segment is full; link new segment if not already linked
            if (tail->next.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == nullptr) {
                Segment* new_segment = new Segment(tail->segment_id + 1);
                Segment* expected_null = nullptr;
                if (!tail->next.compare_exchange_strong(
                        expected_null, new_segment,
                        std::memory_order_release, std::memory_order_acquire)) {
                    // Another producer linked a segment, discard speculative allocation
                    delete new_segment;
                }
            }

            // Advance tail_segment pointer
            Segment* next_seg = tail->next.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
            if (next_seg != nullptr) {
                tail_segment_.compare_exchange_weak(
                    tail, next_seg,
                    std::memory_order_release, std::memory_order_acquire);
            }
        }
    }

    void enqueue(const T& value) {
        emplace(value);
    }

    void enqueue(T&& value) {
        emplace(std::move(value));
    }

    bool try_dequeue(T& out) {
        ebr::Guard guard;
        while (true) {
            Segment* head = head_segment_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
            size_t ticket = head->dequeue_idx.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_acq_rel);

            if (ticket < SegmentSize) {
                Slot& slot = head->slots[ticket];
                const size_t expected_turn = ticket + 1;

                while (true) {
                    size_t turn = slot.turn.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
                    if (turn == expected_turn) {
                        break; // Data ready
                    }

                    // If producers have not published this slot yet, check if queue is empty
                    size_t prod_ticket = head->enqueue_idx.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
                    if (ticket >= prod_ticket) {
                        // Queue empty or lagging producer; undo dequeue ticket attempt
                        // Return false to prevent deadlocks on empty polls
                        return false;
                    }

                    #if defined(__aarch64__)
                    asm volatile("yield" ::: "memory");
                    #elif defined(__x86_64__)
                    asm volatile("pause" ::: "memory");
                    #else
                    std::this_thread::yield();
                    #endif
                }

                // Transfer value and destroy slot contents
                out = std::move(*slot.value_ptr());
                slot.destroy();

                // Slot is now recycled for the next cycle
                slot.turn.store(ticket + SegmentSize, std::memory_order_release);
                return true;
            }

            // Current head segment is fully consumed. Try to advance to next segment
            Segment* next_seg = head->next.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
            if (next_seg == nullptr) {
                // No further segments available
                return false;
            }

            // Atomically advance head segment
            if (head_segment_.compare_exchange_strong(
                    head, next_seg,
                    std::memory_order_release, std::memory_order_acquire)) {
                // Hand over exhausted segment to EBR for safe reclamation
                guard.retire(head);
            }
        }
    }

    std::optional<T> try_dequeue() {
        T out;
        if (try_dequeue(out)) {
            return std::make_optional(std::move(out));
        }
        return std::nullopt;
    }

    [[nodiscard]] bool empty() const noexcept {
        ebr::Guard guard;
        Segment* head = head_segment_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        Segment* tail = tail_segment_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        size_t d_idx = head->dequeue_idx.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        size_t e_idx = tail->enqueue_idx.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        return (head == tail) && (d_idx >= e_idx || d_idx >= SegmentSize);
    }

private:
    alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<Segment*> head_segment_;
    alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size) std::atomic<Segment*> tail_segment_;
};
```

